   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8cd44440-5d0c-473e-b1af-f533559417f3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Q4. How does DBSCAN clustering handle outliers in a dataset?\n",
    "## Answer"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d609a918-73a3-4a0e-b7f6-fc41f6a91eee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### How it works:\n",
    "- DBSCAN groups points that are densely packed together.\n",
    "- Points that don’t have enough neighbors within a certain distance (ε) are labeled as noise.\n",
    "- These noise points are not assigned to any cluster — they’re treated as outliers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "875b7c1e-bb4c-4103-be29-799e44e15c23",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dc3eeda1-e848-4245-a917-4adab2f9323f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Q9. Can DBSCAN clustering be used for semi-supervised learning tasks?\n",
    "## Answer"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e60d6c2f-00d7-4647-88e3-7c97bccabedd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Yes — DBSCAN can support semi-supervised learning, especially in the preprocessing or pseudo-labeling stage.\n",
    "#### ✅ How DBSCAN Helps:\n",
    "- Cluster unlabeled data: \n",
    "    - DBSCAN groups similar data points based on density.\n",
    "- Assign pseudo-labels: \n",
    "    - You can treat each cluster as a class and assign labels to unlabeled data.\n",
    "- Train a supervised model: \n",
    "    - Combine the original labeled data with the pseudo-labeled data to improve learning.\n",
    "#### 🧠 Example Workflow:\n",
    "- Start with 100 labeled and 1,000 unlabeled data points.\n",
    "- Use DBSCAN to cluster the 1,000 unlabeled points.\n",
    "- Assign cluster IDs as temporary labels (ignore noise points).\n",
    "- Merge with the labeled data and train a classifier (e.g., SVM, Random Forest)."
   ]
  },
